+54	scanParens(32::R,D,M,VS) => scanParens(R,0,0,VS++[M])	definitional summary	code 32; disjoint from 40,41,owise	Matches the submitted space branch including empty groups and structurally descends.	loop invariant and parsedParens
+56	scanParens(C::R,D,M,VS) => scanParens(R,D,M,VS) [owise]	definitional summary	all other integer codes, only after specific cases fail	Matches the submitted final implicit no-op branch and structurally descends. This case is unreachable under parenInput but is globally truthful.	loop invariant and parsedParens
+63	scanDepth(scanDone(D,_,_)) => D	definitional projection	scanDone constructor	True projection; scanParens reduces structurally to scanDone for every finite constructor IntSeq.	loop invariant
+64	scanMaximum(scanDone(_,M,_)) => M	definitional projection	scanDone constructor	True projection; same totality argument as scanDepth.	loop invariant and parsedParens
+65	scanValues(scanDone(_,_,VS)) => VS	definitional projection	scanDone constructor	True projection; same totality argument as scanDepth.	loop invariant and parsedParens
+68	finalChar(empty,OLD) => OLD	definitional summary	empty IntSeq; disjoint from cons case	Matches Python for-loop target retention on an empty iterable.	loop invariant
+69	finalChar(C::R,_) => finalChar(R,str(C))	definitional summary	nonempty IntSeq; disjoint from empty and structurally descending	Matches successive assignment of the one-character loop target.	loop invariant
+73	parsedParens(S) => scanValues(scan(S,0,0,[])) ++ [scanMaximum(scan(S,0,0,[]))]	definitional summary	all finite IntSeq; sole equation	Truthfully denotes the submitted algorithm, including its unconditional final append. It does not denote the canonical function on empty or repeated/edge spaces.	entry postcondition
+79	parenInput(empty) => true	definitional predicate	empty IntSeq; disjoint from cons	True base case. Both entry claims therefore have an explicit satisfying input.	both claims
+80	parenInput(C::R) => (C==40 or C==41 or C==32) and parenInput(R)	definitional predicate	nonempty IntSeq; disjoint from empty; structurally descending	Exhaustive alphabet predicate. It does not require balanced groups or single separators, so the theorem is broader than the natural contract but remains truthful about the copied body.	both claims

